# Commits and PRs

Commit **only** when the user asks. Never amend a pushed commit. Never force-push `main`. Never `--no-verify`.

## Subject (mandatory shape)

```
type(scope): lowercase imperative summary
```

- Conventional Commits. **Lowercase** after the colon. Imperative. ≤72 characters. No trailing period.
- One type, one logical change. Do not stack `feat` + `refactor` + `chore` in one commit.
- No `wip:`, `temp:`, `misc:`, or subject lines that just list files.

| Type | When |
|------|------|
| `feat` | user-visible capability |
| `fix` | bug |
| `refactor` | same behavior, clearer structure |
| `test` | tests only |
| `docs` | README, architecture, ADRs, Cursor rules |
| `ci` | GitHub Actions |
| `chore` | ignore files, deps, tooling with no product change |

Scopes: `engine`, `ui`, `chat`. Omit scope when repo-wide (`docs:`, `ci:`).

```
feat(engine): follow renames via git log --follow
fix(ui): keep the selected file across frames
docs: document cache deletion invariance
```

## Body

Wrap at 72 columns. Explain **why** (the surprising git/UI fact), not a file inventory. Blank line after the subject.

## PRs and changelog

PR body follows `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`. `make check` must pass. New git parsing requires a fixture that pins the byte format.

User-visible changes: `CHANGELOG.md` (Keep a Changelog, past tense, under the current version heading). Skip for pure `chore`/`ci`/`test` unless a user would notice.
